Q: How do I make an IRIS list/array property queryable per-element in SQL, without updates rewriting the whole list? A: <p><code>SQLPROJECTION = &quot;table&quot;</code> only changes the SQL view. Storage is still one <code>$ListBuild</code> string in a single node, so every save rewrites the whole thing. There's no parameter that changes that.</p>
<p>For per-element storage you need a real child relationship.</p>
<pre><code>Class CRM.Phone Extends %Persistent
{
Property Contact As CRM.Contact [ Cardinality = parent, Inverse = Phones ];
Property Number As %String(MAXLEN = 30);
Index NumberIdx On Number;
}
</code></pre>
<p>For <code>Tags</code>, keep the list and skip the projection. Add a collection index if you just need existence checks.</p>
<pre><code>Index TagsIdx On Tags(ELEMENTS);
</code></pre>
<p>Above indexes each element without splitting storage, so lookups work and saves stay cheap.</p>

Q: How to use a public struct defined in a source file of /src folder from a test.rs file in /tests folder? A: <p>I've found the good directive, that the compiler eventually suggested me, even if I don't really understand where the <code>catalog_client_client</code> might come from...</p>
<p>Be aware that testing OpenAPI generator methods like I'm doing involves calling <code>async</code> methods, and it's not easy to handle them in a test. Changing the test to a <code>#[tokio::test]</code> was a solution.</p>
<pre class="lang-rs prettyprint-override"><code>mod test {
    use catalog_client_client::apis::configuration::Configuration;
    use catalog_client_client::apis::service_api;

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn list_all_cfs_service_inventories() {
        let cfg = generic_configuration();

        let result = service_api::list_service(&amp;cfg, None, None, None).await;
        println!(&quot;{:#?}&quot;, result);
    }

   fn generic_configuration() -&gt; Configuration {
       let mut cfg = Configuration::new();
       cfg.base_path = &quot;http://localhost:7878/context/serviceInventoryManagement/v5.0&quot;.to_owned();
       println!(&quot;{:#?}&quot;, cfg);

       return cfg;
   }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How do I generate random bytes and convert to hex A: <p>If your goal are just to generate such a hex-string using random numbers, you can do something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'dart:math';

void main() {
  print(randomHexString(32)); // 1401280aa29717e4940f0845f0d43abd
}

Random _random = Random();

String randomHexString(int length) {
  StringBuffer sb = StringBuffer();
  for (var i = 0; i &lt; length; i++) {
    sb.write(_random.nextInt(16).toRadixString(16));
  }
  return sb.toString();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Please use <code>Random.secure()</code> in case you need this for any scenario where you need  cryptographically secure random generation.</p>
